    "result_text": "Done. The build is green: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` / `BUILD SUCCESS`. The two remaining MapStruct warnings (`sharesHousehold`, `customerCode`) are pre-existing and unrelated; the `householdId` warning is gone.\n\n## What I changed and why\n\nI agreed with both findings \u2014 and in fact the derived-on-model approach also fixes a latent bug \u2014 so I did the fuller refactor rather than the minimum.\n\n**Finding 1 (misleading `@Transactional(readOnly = true)`): agreed.** Rather than just drop the annotation, I removed `assignHousehold`/`generateHouseholdId` entirely, since finding 2's refactor subsumes it.\n\n**Finding 2 (derive `householdId` on the model): agreed.**\n- `Owner.getHouseholdId()` is now a `@Transient` derived value sitting right next to `getIdentityKey()`, computing the 12-hex SHA-256 of `normalizedLastName + '|' + postcode` from the owner's own fields. The stored `@Column` field and its setter are gone, and `getIdentityKey()` now reads the derived value.\n- `ClinicServiceImpl`: deleted `assignHousehold` and `generateHouseholdId`; `isHouseholdInUse(Owner)` already compares `getHouseholdId()`, so it just works.\n- `ClinicService`: removed `assignHousehold` from the interface and refreshed the `isHouseholdInUse` Javadoc.\n- `OwnerRestControllerV1`: dropped the `assignHousehold(owner)` call and the \"assign it before the identity key is computed\" ordering comment \u2014 the temporal coupling is gone.\n- `OwnerMapper`: removed `@Mapping(target = \"householdId\", ignore = true)`, which would no longer compile against a read-only property.\n\n**Bonus correctness:** because the id is now a pure function of the stored last name and postcode, a future `updateOwner` that changes either field automatically reflects the new household, instead of leaving a stale stored value.\n\n**One thing I deliberately left:** the `household_id` column still exists in the four `schema.sql` files. It's now unused (nullable, never written), harmless under the test's `ddl-auto=none`, and removing it across four DB dialects I can't exercise here would be scope creep beyond the Java-structure findings. Worth a follow-up cleanup if desired.",
